  we will utilize the tbi info platform to  make sure that the logic is clear  because it provides a number of  resources that will also  guide you as you try to apply these  techniques to a data set of your own  you will also have access to interactive  plots that you can explore  and downstream interpretation including  pathway analysis gene set enrichment  analysis gene ontologies and others  as well as interactive outputs for  exploring the  types of mapping techniques that we're  going to utilize  and also exploratory analysis methods  in the era of bulk rna-seq becoming more  accessible the prominence of machine  learning has  increased and so a lot of people  leverage big data sets like the cancer  genome atlas  and others to explore associations that  are more complex than what you know  typically could be done with  differential gene expression or linear  regression and so we will look at a  number of machine learning methods  specifically applicable to continuous  data used in rna-seq and there we will  look at decision trees  different types of discriminant analysis  and classification methods like random  forest  now when we transition to single cell  transcriptomics we will discuss the  common methods of data preparation as  well as common methods used for analysis  of single cell rna-seq including  dimensionality reduction umap t-sne and  others and pseudotime trajectories so  that we understand both technically how  those should be performed but also in  terms of biological interpretability how  do we actually use some of these methods  to get the types of plots and results  that typically are presented in research  articles  as i mentioned all of the programs will  be supplemented by project examples  these include analysis of  different types of  projects and these projects are going to  range from cancer to infectious diseases  and that brings me to our next program  bioinformatics for infectious diseases  which is going to cover um some overlap  of topics it will include transcriptomic  data as well but it will focus  specifically on the way bioinformatics  is used to study uh the actual pathogen  as well as host pathogen interaction and  so that is why we'll talk about analysis  first of all of genomic data  and also analysis of the host pathogen  interaction uh in genomic and  transcriptomic data so we'll talk about  topics like sequence variation and  alignments evolutionary and phylogenetic  analysis functionality and protein  structure how do we map variants onto  the protein  structure excuse me  how do we analyze 3d structures of  proteins and then transcriptomic  analysis of host response so to do that  we kind of first have to start maybe by  talking about what is the difference  between chronic diseases and infectious  diseases  and  the main difference of course is that  infectious diseases are caused directly  by a pathogen and they spread and the  chronic diseases are caused by  environmental and lifestyle choices and  they typically do not spread and so  because of that we will take examples  that will help us understand how these  pathogens are different  and and how do they actually cause what  we cause what we call disease  and what are the specific functions that  we would like to understand  now the study of  pathogens of course is one of the first  interests in microbiology but also  it is a first example of bioinformatics  leveraged to actually in almost real  time  analyze and understand the evo evolution  of the pathogen as it spreads in a  population so the first example of a  very successful utilization of  bioinformatics in the context of an  epidemic was in the 2014  ebola outbreak even though  bioinformatics obviously has been used  before but during that time in a very  short period of time researchers were  able to  track the evolution as well as design  interventions that are both population  level and  personalized  vaccines and antivirals that became  successful and are now being used to  kind of address these outbreaks so we're  going to use that example as one of the  starting examples of how we can use  bioinformatics effectively to do that  but beyond that we will obviously use  the current pandemic as one of the  examples that is still ongoing  we'll talk about how in this pandemic  specifically  different types of methods have been  used to understand  the interaction between the host and the  pathogen how does the pathogen enter the  cell where can we find the specific  mutations responsible for adaptation how  can we identify protein targets how can  we think about the intervention and  think about the relationships between  the different strains of pathogens  now to do that we will try to understand  different types of sequencing techniques  that have been utilized in the different  examples that i just mentioned  and obviously the specific needs for the  analysis that we will discuss and we  will talk about how these different  types of data are used where where to  find them where can we find collections  of data about these different types of  diseases and what are some both open  source and kind of  available online tools that people use  and what are some of the methods that  are more custom and how to ask questions  from these types of data sets  so bioinformatics for infectious  diseases  will include  kind of a range of topics and a range of  different types of data  so that you can access all of these  different tools  the final program that i want to talk  about is called the research fellowship  the research fellowship is a different  type of a program it does not have a set  curriculum because really the objective  of this program is to provide training  in the context of developing an  independent research project  so to develop an independent research  project your objective is to actually  design a research poster or a project  that you could present to others and so  with that objective in mind what we will  do is we will have access to big data  mentorship and training  and that will be divided up into several  different topics or domains and so these  domains include precision medicine like  cancer neuroscience and others  population health like infectious  diseases biotechnology and agriculture  or even space and astrobiology so  the way that you can design and complete  a project during the short period of  time is by following pre-design steps  on the process of identifying a question  finding a data set that you could ask  that question from  knowing how to analyze the data to  produce the results that you need and  then presentation or how do you  summarize your findings in such a way  that people understand how you arrived  at those conclusions and what are the  actual research findings that you're  presenting  so it all starts with  literature review many of you probably  already know how to do a literature  review but in the context of  bioinformatics literature review  involves several additional steps first  of all we do want to find existing  resources that could be categorized in  databases maybe pipelines or  examples of methods and how they were  applied  in many cases you're looking for gaps  controversy and debate and so a lot of  people in the domain of bioinformatics  compare and contrast different pipelines  maybe evaluate the  comprehensiveness of a resource or a  database and in many cases you can then  uh identify a question or hypothesis  specifically structured around the area  of your interest or your expertise  and then our program will involve  several different steps  including an introduction which will  give you an overview of different types  of methods and tools that you can use  evaluation so that you can propose a  project and get feedback on that  proposal and then practice so that  whenever you are ready to perform the  analysis you're actually understanding  and you're equipped to be able to do  that independently so the outcome of  that is to complete a project and submit  it on our portal and to get there you  will have access to mentorship  someone to review your proposal  technical support from our team and a  community of like-minded students that  you can interact with and potentially  design even group projects together with  them  so on the portal you will be able to  utilize our system to guide you through  all of the steps that are typically  included in preparing a poster  presentation and here i have a brief  recording of how this is possible to do  on the portal itself so the first step  would be to complete your profile  then you would have to add your bio and  connect to your org id or your social  media  and after you do that you will have to  earn 10 000 points by practicing  different types of analyses that we  describe in programs and courses and  complete a course  that speaks about the way that you can  utilize bioinformatics to do a research  project  now after that you can start your own  project  and the project will have several  different sections including an overview  kind of reproducible projects include a  reference data set that is curated and  prepared  so we will go through the process of  submitting that data set on dataverse  and explaining the metadata  we will then talk about all the standard  sections like abstract introduction  methods results discussion and  conclusion including a list of  references and acknowledgments at the  end  now as a result of completing such a  project you will be able to add that  project to your resume or present it at  a poster conference  and so here we have several examples of  people that have submitted either as a  preprint  others have published in the  undergraduate  journals  and others have even used  some of the results that they were  working on in their ongoing research  studies  some just use that example to  train  and acquire skills as well  but what's important is that at the end  of the summer  in august we will have our virtual omics  research symposium  this symposium is a bi-annual event that  we organize together with our partners  where we invite experts from  bioinformatics research  in industry and academia as well as  experts in bioinformatics education to  discuss pressing topics and advances in  bioinformatics but also we provide a  platform for students faculty and  researchers to present those posters and  get feedback specific feedback on their  projects and how to take those projects  further so it's really an opportunity to  find mentors and communities that you  can join and learn about how to  implement these this kind of training um  wherever you are  so again with that i am happy to take  any questions that you might have  i will briefly mention the process of uh  again registration  so for any one of you  that is coming from nepy from the  national association of idea principal  investigators  one of your questions might be  well how do i know that i am from nepea  right how do i know that i am affiliated  with the nappy institution the way to  know that is to go to  nepi.org  which is the website that lists out  all of the institutions  and all of the states  that participate in this program  so if just scroll down a little bit you  will see this map  on the map if you click on any one of  these you will find  a description  and if you scroll down beyond that you  will also see all of the active  principal investigators  so to participate in the program from  nepy  you have to be a member of the  institution where the pi is at  right so if your  lab pi  is a member of nepe that's great  if in your institution there is api that  is enough right so really this is just  the institution so for example in  louisiana  people from lsu  lsu hsc  et cetera now we only have a limited  number of nepy licenses that are going  to be covered a hundred percent  and that's 50.  and then we have an additional 100 that  are going to be offered at 75 off so  that's still